{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"de5a53b746a2784e","name":"card_event_case_yellow","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/WorldAtWar/events/card_event_case_yellow","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"CASE YELLOW","text":"Deal 1 damage. Your units get +1+0. The enemy discards a card.","flavorText":"Case Yellow (German: Fall Gelb), also known as the Manstein Plan or Operation Sichelschnitt, was the German war plan during the Battle of France in 1940. Erich Von Manstein, who was chief of staff of Army Group A of the Wehrmacht, had first formulated the plan for the invasion of France and the Low Countries in October 1939. The plan went through several revisions before it was executed and one of the reasons was that in January 1940 a German aircraft carrying documents with parts of the plan crashed in Belgium and the documents were captured by the Allies in the so-called Mechelen Incident. By this time Manstein took planning and the final version of the plan was devised by Franz Halder who was chief of staff of the Army High Command (OKH). Finally the invasion plan was executed on May 10 1940, and although it was considered risky and opposed by many generals, it led to a swift victory which surprised everyone, even the Germans.","faction":"Germany","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":5,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_case_yellow","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_case_yellow_title","SourceString":"CASE YELLOW","LocalizedString":"CASE YELLOW"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":5,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_case_yellow_text","SourceString":"Deal 1 damage. Your units get +1+0. The enemy discards a card.","LocalizedString":"Deal 1 damage. Your units get +1+0. The enemy discards a card."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_case_yellow_history","SourceString":"Case Yellow (German: Fall Gelb), also known as the Manstein Plan or Operation Sichelschnitt, was the German war plan during the Battle of France in 1940. Erich Von Manstein, who was chief of staff of Army Group A of the Wehrmacht, had first formulated the plan for the invasion of France and the Low Countries in October 1939. The plan went through several revisions before it was executed and one of the reasons was that in January 1940 a German aircraft carrying documents with parts of the plan crashed in Belgium and the documents were captured by the Allies in the so-called Mechelen Incident. By this time Manstein took planning and the final version of the plan was devised by Franz Halder who was chief of staff of the Army High Command (OKH). Finally the invasion plan was executed on May 10 1940, and although it was considered risky and opposed by many generals, it led to a swift victory which surprised everyone, even the Germans.","LocalizedString":"Case Yellow (German: Fall Gelb), also known as the Manstein Plan or Operation Sichelschnitt, was the German war plan during the Battle of France in 1940. Erich Von Manstein, who was chief of staff of Army Group A of the Wehrmacht, had first formulated the plan for the invasion of France and the Low Countries in October 1939. The plan went through several revisions before it was executed and one of the reasons was that in January 1940 a German aircraft carrying documents with parts of the plan crashed in Belgium and the documents were captured by the Allies in the so-called Mechelen Incident. By this time Manstein took planning and the final version of the plan was devised by Franz Halder who was chief of staff of the Army High Command (OKH). Finally the invasion plan was executed on May 10 1940, and although it was considered risky and opposed by many generals, it led to a swift victory which surprised everyone, even the Germans."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::WorldAtWar"